<header class="lesson">
  <div class="crumb">Lesson 0012 &middot; cpp_games / docs / learning</div>
  <h1><code>emplace_back</code> vs. <code>push_back</code></h1>
  <p class="why">Why this matters: your code uses <code class="inline">push_back</code> in 20+ places and <code class="inline">emplace_back</code> in zero. Online lore says "always prefer emplace_back &mdash; it's faster." That lore is wrong in 2026, and reaching for it without understanding has a real safety cost. This lesson is the rule for picking right.</p>
</header>

<h2>1. The concept &mdash; one constructs, the other appends</h2>

<p>Both add a new element to the end of a vector. The difference is <em>how</em> the element gets there.</p>

<ul>
  <li><b><code class="inline">push_back(t)</code></b> takes an already-existing <code class="inline">T</code> (or something implicitly convertible) and copies or moves it into the vector's storage. The function signature is concrete: <code class="inline">void push_back(const T&amp;)</code> and <code class="inline">void push_back(T&amp;&amp;)</code>.</li>
  <li><b><code class="inline">emplace_back(args...)</code></b> takes any number of constructor arguments and forwards them &mdash; perfectly &mdash; to <code class="inline">T</code>'s constructor, building the object <em>directly inside</em> the vector's storage. No temporary is built, no copy or move happens.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The visual step-by-step</h3>

<div class="step-diagram">  push_back(std::string("hello"))                emplace_back("hello")
  ────────────────────────────                  ──────────────────────
  1. construct temp std::string("hello")        1. forward "hello" as constructor arg
     on the stack                                  no temp built
  2. push_back moves it into vector storage     2. emplace_back constructs std::string
     (move constructor runs)                       directly into vector storage
  3. destroy the temp                           3. ... that's it.

  Two constructions + one move.                 One construction. No move.</div>

<p>That sounds like a clear win for <code class="inline">emplace_back</code>. The reality in 2026 is more nuanced: for modern types with cheap move constructors (basically everything in your code), the difference is invisible. The cost of "construct then move" a <code class="inline">std::string</code> is ~5 ns. You can't measure it from a game loop.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <h3>The modern rule of thumb</h3>
  <p><b>Prefer <code class="inline">push_back</code> by default.</b> Reach for <code class="inline">emplace_back</code> when you have a specific reason: you'd construct a temporary anyway, the type has multiple constructor arguments and you want to forward them in place, or there's no move/copy available. The "always emplace" advice you may have seen is older than the language is now &mdash; it predates good move semantics and modern compiler optimisation.</p>
</div>

<h2>2. Minimal example &mdash; three patterns side by side</h2>

<pre class="code"><span class="kw">struct</span> ChatTurn { std::string role; std::string content; };

std::vector&lt;ChatTurn&gt; history;

<span class="com">// (A) "I already have a ChatTurn" — push_back is the obvious choice.</span>
ChatTurn t{<span class="str">"user"</span>, <span class="str">"hello"</span>};
<span class="ok">history.push_back(std::move(t));      <span class="com">// move t into the vector. One move.</span></span>

<span class="com">// (B) "I want to build a new one inline from arguments."</span>
<span class="com">//     push_back constructs a temp + moves it; emplace_back builds in place.</span>
<span class="com">//     For ChatTurn this is unobservable; for types with no cheap move it matters.</span>
<span class="ok">history.push_back({<span class="str">"user"</span>, <span class="str">"hello"</span>});       <span class="com">// build temp, move into vector</span>
history.emplace_back(<span class="str">"user"</span>, <span class="str">"hello"</span>);    <span class="com">// build in place</span></span>

<span class="com">// (C) The DANGEROUS case — explicit constructors.</span>
<span class="kw">struct</span> Capacity { <span class="kw">explicit</span> Capacity(<span class="typ">int</span> n) : value(n) {} <span class="typ">int</span> value; };
std::vector&lt;Capacity&gt; caps;

<span class="bad">caps.push_back(<span class="num">42</span>);        <span class="com">// COMPILE ERROR — push_back refuses explicit conversions.</span>
caps.emplace_back(<span class="num">42</span>);     <span class="com">// COMPILES — emplace_back DOES invoke explicit constructors.</span>
<span class="com">// If you intended 'a Capacity holding 42', emplace_back was right.</span>
<span class="com">// If you intended 'push the integer 42' (and forgot the type),</span>
<span class="com">// emplace_back silently obliged. push_back would have caught the bug.</span></span></pre>

<p>Pattern (A) is what your code does already. Pattern (B) is where <em>both</em> are fine and the choice is style. Pattern (C) is the gotcha &mdash; <b>emplace_back invokes explicit constructors that push_back refuses</b>. That's a feature when you want it and a silent bug when you don't.</p>

<div class="callout bug">
  <h3>The hidden gotcha &mdash; explicit constructors</h3>
  <p>The Chromium project publishes an internal PSA: <em>"Prefer push_back() over emplace_back() unless you know you need it."</em> The reason is exactly pattern (C). <code class="inline">emplace_back</code> is more permissive about what it'll convert to <code class="inline">T</code>; <code class="inline">push_back</code> is stricter. Sometimes the permissiveness is what you want; usually you don't want a "fast write" function to be slipping in conversions you didn't ask for.</p>
</div>

<h2>3. In your project &mdash; every <code class="inline">push_back</code>, audited</h2>

<h3>The "could be emplace" cases &mdash; equivalent in practice</h3>

<small class="path">cpp_game_with_llm_npcs/src/Npc.cpp:30</small>
<pre class="code">history_.push_back({<span class="str">"user"</span>, std::move(pendingUserLine_)});
history_.push_back({<span class="str">"assistant"</span>, reply.content});</pre>

<p>The brace-init constructs a temporary <code class="inline">ChatTurn</code> and <code class="inline">push_back</code> moves it in. <code class="inline">emplace_back("user", std::move(pendingUserLine_))</code> would build in place. For a struct of two <code class="inline">std::string</code>s with their cheap moves, the difference is unmeasurable. The <code class="inline">push_back</code> form is also more readable &mdash; the braces make the "I am building a ChatTurn" intent visible, while <code class="inline">emplace_back</code> hides the type.</p>

<small class="path">cpp_racing_game/src/main.cpp:285</small>
<pre class="code">pts.push_back(sf::Vector2f(cx + std::cos(a) * r, cy + std::sin(a) * r));</pre>

<p>This is the textbook "build a temporary and shove it in" case. <code class="inline">pts.emplace_back(cx + std::cos(a) * r, cy + std::sin(a) * r)</code> would forward those two floats straight to <code class="inline">sf::Vector2f</code>'s constructor. Both compile, both produce the same machine code on modern compilers. <code class="inline">emplace_back</code> is arguably cleaner here &mdash; no redundant <code class="inline">sf::Vector2f(...)</code> spelled out. Either is fine.</p>

<h3>The "emplace would be wrong" cases &mdash; you're already moving</h3>

<small class="path">cpp_game_with_llm_npcs/src/LlmClient.cpp:62</small>
<pre class="code">replies_.push_back(std::move(reply));</pre>

<small class="path">cpp_game_with_llm_npcs/src/DialogUI.cpp:98</small>
<pre class="code">transcript_.push_back(std::move(line));</pre>

<p>Here you already have an existing object (<code class="inline">reply</code>, <code class="inline">line</code>) and you're transferring ownership into the vector. <code class="inline">push_back(std::move(x))</code> is exactly the right shape. Writing <code class="inline">emplace_back(std::move(reply))</code> would call <code class="inline">ChatReply</code>'s move constructor with one argument &mdash; same outcome, but the intent ("I'm appending this thing") is muddier. The Abseil style guide says it explicitly: use <code class="inline">push_back</code> when you have an existing object to put in.</p>

<h3>The "definitely fine as push_back" cases &mdash; copies of locals</h3>

<small class="path">cpp_shooter_game/src/main.cpp:410, 498, 555, 568, 748</small>
<pre class="code">game.impacts.push_back(fx);
game.pickups.push_back(pickup);
game.projectiles.push_back(shot);
game.enemies.push_back(enemy);</pre>

<p>Each appends a copy of a local <code class="inline">struct</code>. Since these are POD-ish (or close to it &mdash; mostly arithmetic types), copy and move are identical. <code class="inline">emplace_back(fx)</code> would just call <code class="inline">ImpactEffect</code>'s copy constructor with one argument &mdash; same code. No reason to change anything; <code class="inline">push_back</code> is the clearer expression of "append this thing."</p>

<div class="callout real">
  <h3>Audit summary</h3>
  <p>Across all 20 <code class="inline">push_back</code> calls in your code, zero of them have a meaningful win from converting to <code class="inline">emplace_back</code>. The racing game's <code class="inline">sf::Vector2f</code> temporary is the closest call &mdash; pure style, no perf. The Npc <code class="inline">ChatTurn</code> calls would also work as emplace, but the brace-init form is more readable. This is what the modern advice means in practice: <em>your default is fine</em>.</p>
</div>

<h2>4. Often confused with...</h2>

<h3><code class="inline">emplace_back</code> vs <code class="inline">push_back</code> (the headline)</h3>

<table class="compare">
  <thead><tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th><code class="inline">push_back</code></th><th><code class="inline">emplace_back</code></th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Signature</td><td><code>void push_back(const T&amp;)</code> + rvalue overload</td><td><code>template&lt;class... Args&gt; void emplace_back(Args&amp;&amp;... args)</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Takes</td><td>An object of type <code class="inline">T</code></td><td>Constructor arguments forwarded to <code class="inline">T</code></td></tr>
    <tr><td>How element is created</td><td>Copy/move the argument</td><td>Construct in place from args</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Explicit ctors</td><td>Refuses (compile error)</td><td>Invokes (silent)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Best when</td><td>You have an existing object (the common case)</td><td>You'd build a temp purely to append it</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Readability</td><td>Higher (type is explicit at call site)</td><td>Lower (call site shows only args; type is hidden in <code>vector&lt;T&gt;</code> declaration)</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3><code class="inline">emplace_back</code> vs <code class="inline">push_back(std::move(x))</code></h3>
<p>These are sometimes presented as identical. They're not. <code class="inline">emplace_back(x)</code> forwards <code class="inline">x</code> to a one-argument constructor of <code class="inline">T</code> &mdash; might be a copy constructor, a move constructor, or some other converting constructor that happens to match. <code class="inline">push_back(std::move(x))</code> is concrete: it calls the rvalue-reference overload and the move constructor. The latter expresses intent more clearly when <em>moving an existing object</em> is what you mean.</p>

<h3><code class="inline">emplace_back</code> vs <code class="inline">make_X</code> functions</h3>
<p>Some libraries have factory functions (<code class="inline">make_pair</code>, <code class="inline">make_tuple</code>, <code class="inline">make_unique</code>). These construct and return an object &mdash; you'd then <code class="inline">push_back</code> the result. <code class="inline">emplace_back</code> skips the make step by constructing directly. For <code class="inline">std::unique_ptr</code> specifically, <code class="inline">vec.emplace_back(std::make_unique&lt;T&gt;(...))</code> is the standard pattern because <code class="inline">unique_ptr</code> isn't copyable; you have to either move it in (<code class="inline">push_back(make_unique...)</code>) or emplace it. Both work.</p>

<h3>The Abseil/Chromium guidance vs the older "always emplace" advice</h3>
<p>Pre-2015 advice was "<code class="inline">emplace_back</code> can skip a move, so prefer it." That advice predates the language's full move-semantics maturity and modern compilers' RVO/copy-elision. The current consensus among industry style guides (Abseil, Chromium, Google C++ Style) is the inverse: <b>prefer <code class="inline">push_back</code>; reach for <code class="inline">emplace_back</code> when you have a specific reason</b>. If you read an older tutorial, calibrate accordingly.</p>

<h3><code class="inline">emplace_back</code> vs <code class="inline">emplace</code> (with no <code class="inline">_back</code>)</h3>
<p>Different operation. <code class="inline">emplace_back</code> is vector/deque-specific and adds at the end. <code class="inline">emplace</code> on <code class="inline">std::vector</code> takes an iterator and inserts in the middle (O(n) for vector). <code class="inline">emplace</code> on <code class="inline">std::map</code>/<code class="inline">std::set</code> inserts into the tree. Different containers, different return types &mdash; don't conflate the names.</p>

<h2>5. Quiz</h2>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">Look at <code class="inline">replies_.push_back(std::move(reply));</code> in <code class="inline">LlmClient.cpp</code>. If you change it to <code class="inline">replies_.emplace_back(std::move(reply));</code>, what happens at runtime?</p>
