  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">You define <code class="inline">struct Bytes { explicit Bytes(size_t n) : count(n) {} size_t count; };</code> and write <code class="inline">std::vector&lt;Bytes&gt; bufs; bufs.emplace_back(1024);</code>. A teammate "improves" the code to <code class="inline">bufs.push_back(1024);</code>. What happens?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Backwards. push_back refuses to invoke explicit constructors implicitly. The compile error happens at push_back; emplace_back was the one that compiled.">
      The push_back version compiles and is faster.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. Bytes' constructor is explicit, so 1024 won't implicitly convert to Bytes. push_back refuses, the compiler errors. To make push_back work you'd write push_back(Bytes(1024)). The original emplace_back was actually the right tool here — explicit constructors are one of the legitimate emplace_back use cases.">
      Compile error on the push_back line &mdash; <code class="inline">Bytes</code>'s constructor is explicit, so <code class="inline">1024</code> won't implicitly convert. <code class="inline">emplace_back</code> was correct.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Both compile and produce the same Bytes element... is wrong because push_back wouldn't compile due to the explicit. The compiler explicitly catches this case.">
      Both compile and produce a <code class="inline">Bytes</code> with <code class="inline">count == 1024</code>.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="explicit affects implicit conversions, not size constants. push_back simply refuses to convert int to an explicit-only Bytes. No special handling of 1024 vs other numbers.">
      Both compile; <code class="inline">push_back(1024)</code> silently truncates if 1024 is larger than what <code class="inline">size_t</code> holds.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">A "modern C++ tutorial" written in 2019 says "always prefer <code class="inline">emplace_back</code> &mdash; it's faster and idiomatic." What's the most accurate critique in 2026?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="emplace_back hasn't been deprecated; it's a fine tool when you have a reason. The critique is about defaults, not removal.">
      It's outdated; <code class="inline">emplace_back</code> was deprecated in C++23.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. Modern industry style guides (Abseil, Chromium, Google C++) flipped the advice: push_back is the default, emplace_back when you have a specific reason. The 2019 advice missed that emplace_back also bypasses explicit constructors (a safety regression for normal use) and that, for modern movable types, the 'avoids a move' win is unmeasurable.">
      The advice is out of date. Current style guides prefer <code class="inline">push_back</code> as the default; <code class="inline">emplace_back</code> can bypass explicit constructors and hide types at the call site.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The standard library hasn't changed semantics — emplace_back still constructs in place. The change is in stylistic advice, not language behaviour.">
      It's wrong because C++23 changed <code class="inline">emplace_back</code> to make a copy first.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="The compiler doesn't issue this kind of warning. The point is that humans should prefer push_back; the compiler is neutral.">
      Compilers now emit a warning when you write <code class="inline">emplace_back</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">Look at the racing game's <code class="inline">pts.push_back(sf::Vector2f(cx + std::cos(a) * r, cy + std::sin(a) * r));</code>. Of the rewrites below, which is the <em>only</em> one that compiles and means the same thing?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="emplace_back wants sf::Vector2f's constructor arguments — pass the two floats directly, not pre-wrapped in sf::Vector2f(...). Wrapping defeats the point and is just push_back with extra steps.">
      <code class="inline">pts.emplace_back(sf::Vector2f(cx + ..., cy + ...));</code>
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. emplace_back forwards args to sf::Vector2f's constructor directly. Two floats in, one sf::Vector2f constructed in place. Same outcome as the original, just no intermediate temp.">
      <code class="inline">pts.emplace_back(cx + std::cos(a) * r, cy + std::sin(a) * r);</code>
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="That's an assignment to the back of the vector, but pts.back() is the *last existing* element — and if pts is empty (which it might be on first iteration), back() is undefined behaviour. Wrong shape entirely.">
      <code class="inline">pts.back() = sf::Vector2f(cx + ..., cy + ...);</code>
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="reserve allocates capacity but doesn't add elements. Without a subsequent push/emplace, no points are added.">
      <code class="inline">pts.reserve(pts.size() + 1); pts[pts.size()] = sf::Vector2f(cx + ..., cy + ...);</code>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">You have a vector of <code class="inline">std::unique_ptr&lt;Backend&gt;</code>. Which line adds a new <code class="inline">OllamaBackend</code> to it?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Backslash. You can't copy a unique_ptr; the line wouldn't compile. Even if you could, you'd construct a unique_ptr separately and push it in, which works but is more code.">
      <code class="inline">backends.push_back(std::unique_ptr&lt;Backend&gt;(<span class="kw">new</span> OllamaBackend(host, port, model)));</code>
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. make_unique builds the unique_ptr; push_back accepts it as an rvalue and moves it into the vector. Idiomatic and exception-safe. (emplace_back(make_unique...(args)) would also work, but you're not gaining anything — there's no temporary to elide.)">
      <code class="inline">backends.push_back(std::make_unique&lt;OllamaBackend&gt;(host, port, model));</code>
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="emplace_back(host, port, model) would try to construct a unique_ptr<Backend> from three arguments — unique_ptr's constructor doesn't take (host, port, model). Compile error.">
      <code class="inline">backends.emplace_back(host, port, model);</code>
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="That tries to assign a unique_ptr to a non-existent element. Even if the vector were non-empty, it wouldn't *add* — it'd overwrite the first element.">
      <code class="inline">backends[<span class="num">0</span>] = std::make_unique&lt;OllamaBackend&gt;(host, port, model);</code>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

